  "response_text": "Changing the scheduler to always run the highest-priority task can lead to problems like starvation, where lower-priority tasks never get a chance to run. If a high-priority task is always ready, it can completely block other tasks from executing. This is unfair and can cause important background or support tasks to be skipped, potentially breaking parts of the system. A better solution would be to use priority scheduling with techniques like aging or time slices to ensure all tasks eventually get CPU time.\\n",
